WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.096
Welcome to Module 2 of The Zero
Trust Assessment as Evidence:

2
00:00:04.096 --> 00:00:06.200
running the assessment safely.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.217
The assessment is read-only, but running it
takes more privilege than that suggests,

4
00:00:11.217 --> 00:00:14.125
and its output is a map of
where the tenant is weak.

5
00:00:14.125 --> 00:00:18.193
In this module you prepare a host,
consent once, run as a reader,

6
00:00:18.193 --> 00:00:23.238
connect in the tenant's own cloud, and
hash the output before anyone opens it.

7
00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:28.065
It covers Steps 2 to 5 of the Zero
Trust Assessment implementation guide,

8
00:00:28.065 --> 00:00:33.010
and takes about thirty-five minutes,
plus Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

9
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:37.910
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:41.960
The assessment only reads, but
running it takes real privilege.

11
00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.586
An administrator consents to the tool's
Microsoft Graph read permissions,

12
00:00:46.586 --> 00:00:49.869
and the run account needs a
set of roles: Global Reader,

13
00:00:49.869 --> 00:00:54.122
Security Reader, Reader on the Azure
subscriptions in scope, and more.

14
00:00:54.122 --> 00:00:57.036
One of them, SharePoint
Administrator, can write.

15
00:00:57.036 --> 00:00:58.860
And the output is sensitive.

16
00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.172
It's a map of where the tenant is weak.

17
00:01:01.172 --> 00:01:05.922
Microsoft's get-started page on Learn says
to share the report and export folder

18
00:01:05.922 --> 00:01:09.592
only with authorized personnel,
to store the report securely,

19
00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:13.334
and to delete the local folder
once the assessment is complete.

20
00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:18.746
So this module treats the run like any
other privileged operation: a clean host,

21
00:01:18.746 --> 00:01:21.909
consent once, a reader
account, the right cloud,

22
00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:25.180
and a hash taken before
anyone opens a file.

23
00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.843
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

24
00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:35.401
You'll prepare a clean host with
PowerShell seven point six,

25
00:01:35.401 --> 00:01:38.379
the pinned module, and a
restricted report folder.

26
00:01:38.379 --> 00:01:43.580
You'll explain who consents, who runs, and
why the two sources' role lists differ.

27
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:47.296
You'll review the delegated
grant the consent leaves behind,

28
00:01:47.296 --> 00:01:49.005
and say when not to revoke it.

29
00:01:49.005 --> 00:01:53.169
You'll connect in Commercial,
G C C, G C C High or D O D,

30
00:01:53.169 --> 00:01:56.905
and confirm the session's
tenant and cloud.

31
00:01:56.905 --> 00:01:59.742
And you'll run the assessment
and hash its export.

32
00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:03.905
You need Module 1, and a skim of
the Privileged Access implementation

33
00:02:03.905 --> 00:02:07.118
guide's pim model, for the
run account's eligible roles.

34
00:02:07.118 --> 00:02:10.840
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

35
00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.165
Unit 1 is the host and the output.

36
00:02:17.165 --> 00:02:19.290
Learn asks for PowerShell seven.

37
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.983
Release three point one point oh's own
guide asks for seven point six or later,

38
00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:26.740
because its pinned
Exchange module needs it,

39
00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.390
and recommends a dedicated,
clean Windows virtual machine.

40
00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.056
On other platforms, the
SharePoint Online and

41
00:02:33.056 --> 00:02:36.002
Azure Information Protection
checks are skipped.

42
00:02:36.002 --> 00:02:40.265
Install the module version the decision
record pins, and nothing newer.

43
00:02:40.265 --> 00:02:43.259
Every run gets a new,
empty folder that only the

44
00:02:43.259 --> 00:02:46.490
run account and the evidence
custodians can read.

45
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:50.395
And one thing people miss: deleting
the report folder doesn't remove

46
00:02:50.395 --> 00:02:54.440
PowerShell's separate logging folder,
which can also hold tenant data.

47
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:59.580
The tool's guide says so, and the program
treats that folder as part of the output.

48
00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:07.605
Unit 2 is consent and roles,
and the two sources don't agree.

49
00:03:07.605 --> 00:03:12.680
Learn's get-started page says the first
connection needs a Global Administrator,

50
00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.649
lists four roles for later runs,

51
00:03:14.649 --> 00:03:17.755
and on the same page says
a Global Reader is enough.

52
00:03:17.755 --> 00:03:22.682
The tool's guide for three point one point
oh says a Cloud Application Administrator

53
00:03:22.682 --> 00:03:26.412
or Application Administrator
can grant the delegated consent,

54
00:03:26.412 --> 00:03:28.805
and that consent assigns no runtime role.

55
00:03:28.805 --> 00:03:32.503
It says the module validates Global
Reader for the Graph context,

56
00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:36.680
and that the SharePoint tenant commands
need SharePoint Administrator.

57
00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.526
Learn lists twenty-one Graph permissions;

58
00:03:39.526 --> 00:03:44.405
Get Z T Graph Scope in three point one
point oh prints thirty-four, all read.

59
00:03:44.405 --> 00:03:49.205
The program follows the tool's guide,
because it describes the version being run.

60
00:03:49.205 --> 00:03:53.192
The consent administrator consents
and doesn't run the assessment.

61
00:03:53.192 --> 00:03:55.739
The run account is a
dedicated reader whose

62
00:03:55.739 --> 00:03:58.920
roles are activated through
pim before each run.

63
00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.409
Consent leaves something behind:
a delegated permission grant,

64
00:04:06.409 --> 00:04:09.020
on the application the
tool signs in through.

65
00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:13.350
By default that's the Microsoft Graph
PowerShell application, which other

66
00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:17.820
scripts in the tenant share, so every
script that uses it inherits the consent.

67
00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:22.545
Learn's page on the Graph PowerShell
authentication commands recommends a custom

68
00:04:22.545 --> 00:04:25.832
application when you need to
isolate the consent it holds,

69
00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.845
and the tool accepts one with
the client I D parameter.

70
00:04:28.845 --> 00:04:32.983
The program uses a dedicated
application; that's design guidance.

71
00:04:32.983 --> 00:04:35.795
Review the grant after
every version change,

72
00:04:35.795 --> 00:04:39.671
and compare its scopes with the
list Get Z T Graph Scope prints.

73
00:04:39.671 --> 00:04:43.827
When the tool is retired, revoke
a dedicated application's grant,

74
00:04:43.827 --> 00:04:45.945
with Learn's revocation command.

75
00:04:45.945 --> 00:04:50.107
Never revoke the shared one without
asking who else relies on it.

76
00:04:50.107 --> 00:04:53.029
Learn also notes that
existing tokens stay valid

77
00:04:53.029 --> 00:04:55.960
for their lifetime after
a grant is deleted.

78
00:04:59.710 --> 00:05:01.185
Unit 3 connects.

79
00:05:01.185 --> 00:05:05.875
Learn's Zero Trust Assessment pages don't
mention government clouds; the tool's

80
00:05:05.875 --> 00:05:10.710
source does, and the guide matches its
parameters to Learn's page for each service.

81
00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.148
Start with G C C, the workbook's baseline.

82
00:05:14.148 --> 00:05:18.004
A G C C tenant uses the worldwide
Graph and Azure endpoints,

83
00:05:18.004 --> 00:05:23.548
so it connects with the default Global
environment, the same command as commercial.

84
00:05:23.548 --> 00:05:26.515
G C C High uses the U S Gov environment,

85
00:05:26.515 --> 00:05:30.472
with the Exchange environment
name for G C C High.

86
00:05:30.472 --> 00:05:36.360
D O D uses U S Gov D O D, with
its own Exchange environment name.

87
00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:40.760
Then confirm the session's tenant
and cloud with Get M G Context.

88
00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.924
Pass the SharePoint admin
U R L in every cloud;

89
00:05:43.924 --> 00:05:47.773
otherwise the tool infers
one from the initial domain.

90
00:05:47.773 --> 00:05:53.447
And in D O D, Learn and the tool give
different Security and Compliance addresses.

91
00:05:53.447 --> 00:05:57.970
If the data pillar's tests come back
disconnected there, check that first.

92
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.730
Here's where each service
stands, cloud by cloud,

93
00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:07.045
from the guide's availability table.

94
00:06:07.045 --> 00:06:13.296
Microsoft Graph: G C C uses the
worldwide graph dot microsoft dot com,

95
00:06:13.296 --> 00:06:19.658
G C C High uses graph dot microsoft dot
U S, and D O D its own D O D graph host.

96
00:06:19.658 --> 00:06:22.337
Exchange Online: no parameter in G C C,

97
00:06:22.337 --> 00:06:25.908
and an environment name
for each of the other two.

98
00:06:25.908 --> 00:06:29.970
Security and Compliance: the
default endpoint in G C C;

99
00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:34.217
a dot U S address in G C C High;
and in D O D, Learn and the tool's

100
00:06:34.217 --> 00:06:39.295
source give different addresses,
and which one works wasn't tested.

101
00:06:39.295 --> 00:06:44.457
Azure: Azure Cloud for G C C, Azure
U S Government for the other two.

102
00:06:44.457 --> 00:06:49.508
SharePoint admin: pass it explicitly;
D O D's isn't yet specified.

103
00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:53.899
Until a run has been proved in your own
cloud, run one pillar at a time in a

104
00:06:53.899 --> 00:06:59.110
test tenant of the same cloud, and keep
ScubaGear as the federal baseline tool.

105
00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.860
Unit 4 is the run itself.

106
00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:08.318
On a large tenant it can take
more than twenty-four hours,

107
00:07:08.318 --> 00:07:10.347
and Microsoft says not to stop it.

108
00:07:10.347 --> 00:07:14.874
The disable-telemetry switch suppresses
the tool's one telemetry event,

109
00:07:14.874 --> 00:07:16.610
which carries the tenant I D.

110
00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:21.172
Whether to use it is the see-so's
decision, written in the decision record.

111
00:07:21.172 --> 00:07:24.623
The no-browser switch keeps the
report from opening on screen.

112
00:07:24.623 --> 00:07:29.694
As soon as the run ends, before
anyone opens or copies a file,

113
00:07:29.694 --> 00:07:32.547
hash the export, S H A two fifty-six.

114
00:07:32.547 --> 00:07:35.773
That hash is what later shows
the output wasn't changed.

115
00:07:35.773 --> 00:07:38.479
And if the run fails,
capture a support package

116
00:07:38.479 --> 00:07:40.759
in the same session, before you close it.

117
00:07:40.759 --> 00:07:42.702
The guide's Step 5 has the command,

118
00:07:42.702 --> 00:07:46.650
and says to write the package
to a separate protected folder.

119
00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:54.512
Lab 2 runs the real tool in a
test tenant, never production.

120
00:07:54.512 --> 00:07:57.437
The authors haven't run
it, as the page says.

121
00:07:57.437 --> 00:08:01.651
You need a test tenant in the cloud
you'll assess, with fictional users;

122
00:08:01.651 --> 00:08:05.421
a consent administrator; a reader
account with Global Reader,

123
00:08:05.421 --> 00:08:09.708
Security Reader and SharePoint
Administrator, eligible through pim;

124
00:08:09.708 --> 00:08:12.813
and a Windows host with
PowerShell seven point six.

125
00:08:12.813 --> 00:08:15.524
Values you supply are in angle brackets.

126
00:08:15.524 --> 00:08:19.090
Step one prepares the host:
check the PowerShell version,

127
00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:23.337
install the pinned module, create
the report folder, and restrict it.

128
00:08:23.337 --> 00:08:26.779
Step two consents once, as
the consent administrator:

129
00:08:26.779 --> 00:08:32.099
print the scopes the tool will request, then
connect to Graph and accept the prompt.

130
00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:35.350
Step three reviews the grant
while you're still signed in.

131
00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:37.955
The scope field lists every permission the

132
00:08:37.955 --> 00:08:40.488
tool's application now
holds in the tenant.

133
00:08:40.488 --> 00:08:45.440
Compare it with the list Get Z T Graph
Scope printed, and keep the output.

134
00:08:49.190 --> 00:08:51.379
Step four connects as the reader,

135
00:08:51.379 --> 00:08:55.515
after activating its roles in
pim, in a new PowerShell session.

136
00:08:55.515 --> 00:09:00.401
The command on the slide is for G C C
and commercial; for G C C High or D O D,

137
00:09:00.401 --> 00:09:04.677
use the connection commands from
the guide's Step 4 instead.

138
00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:08.440
Then confirm the session's
tenant, environment and account.

139
00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.978
Step five runs the assessment
and hashes the export.

140
00:09:11.978 --> 00:09:15.714
Keep the report folder:
Modules 3 and 4 read it.

141
00:09:15.714 --> 00:09:18.990
Step six backs it all out,
when you finish the path.

142
00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:23.265
Disconnect, and remove the reader's
role assignments in the test tenant.

143
00:09:23.265 --> 00:09:27.205
If you consented on a dedicated
application, remove its grant as a

144
00:09:27.205 --> 00:09:31.290
Cloud Application Administrator,
with the grant I D from step three.

145
00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.610
Don't revoke the shared
Microsoft Graph PowerShell

146
00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:37.390
grant if anything else
in the tenant uses it.

147
00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:39.580
Then delete the report folder.

148
00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.941
The federal requirement, from NIST special

149
00:09:45.941 --> 00:09:49.205
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

150
00:09:49.205 --> 00:09:51.430
C M six, configuration settings:

151
00:09:51.430 --> 00:09:56.480
the host's pinned module and PowerShell
versions, recorded for each run.

152
00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.658
A C six, least privilege: read
roles only, activated just in time,

153
00:10:00.658 --> 00:10:04.917
with the consent administrator
separate from the run account;

154
00:10:04.917 --> 00:10:10.242
the evidence is the role assignments, the
pim activations, and the grant review.

155
00:10:10.242 --> 00:10:14.850
C A two and C A seven: the assessment
run, in the tenant's own cloud;

156
00:10:14.850 --> 00:10:19.367
the evidence is the connection
context and the export's hash.

157
00:10:19.367 --> 00:10:22.498
And A U nine, protection
of audit information:

158
00:10:22.498 --> 00:10:25.630
hashes taken before
anyone opened the files.

159
00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:29.917
Each row lines up with a fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicator.

160
00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:34.080
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

161
00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.805
Check yourself with two questions.

162
00:10:39.805 --> 00:10:43.008
First: Learn lists twenty-one
Graph permissions,

163
00:10:43.008 --> 00:10:47.493
and Get Z T Graph Scope in three
point one point oh prints thirty-four.

164
00:10:47.493 --> 00:10:50.593
Which list does the consent
review compare against?

165
00:10:50.593 --> 00:10:52.828
The answer is b: Get Z T Graph Scope's,

166
00:10:52.828 --> 00:10:56.342
because it's what the version
you're running requests.

167
00:10:56.342 --> 00:11:00.530
Second: a G C C tenant connects
with which environment value?

168
00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:06.905
The answer is a, Global, because G C C uses
the worldwide Graph and Azure endpoints.

169
00:11:06.905 --> 00:11:12.480
U S Gov is for G C C High,
and U S Gov D O D for D O D.

170
00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:17.750
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

171
00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:22.325
To sum up.

172
00:11:22.325 --> 00:11:26.875
You prepared a clean host with the pinned
module and a restricted report folder.

173
00:11:26.875 --> 00:11:29.787
You consented once, as
a consent administrator,

174
00:11:29.787 --> 00:11:32.700
and reviewed the grant
the consent left behind.

175
00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:36.725
You connected in the tenant's
own cloud, starting with G C C.

176
00:11:36.725 --> 00:11:39.127
And you ran the assessment as a reader and

177
00:11:39.127 --> 00:11:41.600
hashed its export before anyone opened it.

178
00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:46.934
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module: the module and PowerShell versions,

179
00:11:46.934 --> 00:11:51.460
the grant review, the reader's role
assignments and pim activations,

180
00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:54.450
the connection context,
and the export's hash.

181
00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.036
Next is Module 3, from
results to a closure register,

182
00:11:58.036 --> 00:12:02.030
where the kit turns an export
into findings with owners.
